# .agent-review — review scope
#
# Four first-class artifacts, and each has to be true for the system to be
# trustworthy:
#
#   ADRs      prescribe the design.
#   Code      implements it.
#   Tests     verify it.
#   Inventory describes what was actually built.
#
# Each section below lists the paths that agent REVIEWS. Section names are
# case-insensitive.
#
# Review scope is allowlist-based. Only paths explicitly included in an
# agent's section are within that agent's standing review scope. All other
# paths are out of scope by default. [Exclude] is an authoritative,
# non-exhaustive denylist that overrides every agent's inclusions. Neither
# omission nor exclusion restricts reading files as evidence.
#
# REVIEW SCOPE IS NOT EVIDENCE SCOPE, and the difference is load-bearing.
#
#   Review scope   what an agent may raise a finding about, judge, or change.
#                  Bounded by the sections below.
#   Evidence scope what an agent may READ to establish whether something is
#                  true. Unbounded.
#
# An agent may read anything at all — another repository, an installed
# package in node_modules, a log, a deployed template — to verify a claim.
# What it may not do is turn a file outside its review scope into a finding.
#
# The metric for adding a path to a review section: does it affect the design,
# the architecture, or the functionality? If not, it is not reviewed. That
# rule decides future entries, so this file should not need re-arguing each
# time something is added to the repository.
#
# ENFORCEMENT IS SHARED. The orchestrator loads this file before invoking an
# agent, so it does not ask for work the file puts out of scope. Each agent
# also reads it independently and is governed by it rather than by the
# orchestrator's account of it. Where an instruction and this file disagree,
# this file wins.
#
# AD-HOC REQUESTS ARE ALLOWED; STANDING CHANGES ARE NOT. The orchestrator may
# ask an agent to look at one specific named file outside its scope for one
# invocation — "review scratch/new-infrastructure-idea.md" is a legitimate
# request. It may not extend an agent's scope for the duration: "also add
# scratch/ to your review scope" is not. A standing change to review scope is
# an edit to this file, and this file is the owner's.

# Archie: does the code conform to the design the ADRs prescribe?
# ADRs are in scope because they ARE the design — read as the governing
# baseline, never rewritten to match the code.
[Archie]
- docs/adr/
- src/
- tests/
- cli/

# Qoala: does it work, and do the tests actually establish that it works?
# tests/ is named separately from src/ deliberately. Tests are a
# first-class artifact, not a subdirectory — a suite that passes while the
# deployment breaks is the failure this review exists to catch, so the tests
# are judged on whether they would fail when the behaviour breaks, not on
# whether they are present and green.
[Qoala]
- src/
- tests/
- cli/
- package.json
- package-lock.json
- .gitignore

# [Exclude] carves a subtree out of an included path. It is for the case where
# a section includes a folder but one branch of it should not be reviewed —
# include `docs/design/`, exclude `docs/design/archive/`. It is not a general
# denylist: a path nobody included is already out of scope, so listing it here
# adds nothing.
[Exclude]
